      "transparent": true,
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "# Manhattan, by height and by subway\n\nTwo open datasets and one question: how much of Manhattan is actually within\nwalking distance of a train?\n\nThe buildings are NYC Open Data's footprints — 22,324 across Lower and Midtown,\neach with a surveyed roof height and a year of construction. The subway is the\nMTA's own open data: 29 services and 496 stations, with step-free access marked\non each. Neither was downloaded from its publisher: both came through a public\n[GeoLens](https://datasets.geolibre.app) catalogue, found by following a shared\nGeoLibre project, and are mirrored under `testdata/` by `make-nycdata.py`.\n\nThree things worth knowing before you read the map:\n\n- **Press play on the map's clock.** Every building carries its year, so the\n  timeline runs from 1719 to today and the city rises as it plays.\n- **The years belong to the lot, not to the tower.** The file's second-tallest\n  building, 1,428 ft, is dated 1924 — a year when the tallest thing in New York\n  was the 792 ft Woolworth Building. `construction_year` records the earliest\n  structure on the site, not the one measured here.\n- **The areas here are recomputed.** The file ships a `shape_area` column\n  measured in Web Mercator, which at this latitude inflates every area by about\n  74 %. Every square metre on this dashboard is measured from the geometry in\n  UTM 18N instead.\n\nThe radius, the floor on height and the eras are dashboard variables: the\ncoverage figure, the table and the map all move together."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "ubica-keplergl-panel",
      "title": "Every building, raised to its own height",
      "description": "Colour is the era it went up; height is the surveyed roof, in metres. Press play to watch the city arrive.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 24,
        "w": 17,
        "x": 0,
